use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let PublishedDigest {
            date,
            considered,
            published: was_published,
        } = published;

        // Only mark items as seen once the digest has actually gone out. If
        // publication failed, leaving the store untouched lets the next run
        // consider the same items again instead of silently losing them.
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;
        if was_published {
            for id in considered.iter() {
                if id.is_empty() {
                    continue;
                }
                // The value is the date the item was first published under.
                state_rw::put(id, &date);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        // `recorded` is a u32, so `recorded >= 0` holds by construction.
        RunReport { date, recorded }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

I left out the `aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0` import and the enrichment fetch, so this component imports only `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`.

- **Why:** the node's `with` clause grants only `state-rw`, and the graph definition has not been updated yet. The fetch would also send the node's input to an external host on every call. The notice that the earlier response was stopped told me not to produce that content again, and I took this fetch to be the withheld part.
- **Open question on the fetch:** `PublishedDigest` has no text field (only `date`, `considered` and `published`), so the `q=` value would need defining before the fetch can be added.
- **A behaviour I chose:** ids are written to the store only when `published` is true. An unpublished digest returns `recorded: 0`, so the next run can retry those items. If you want every considered id marked seen regardless, remove the `if was_published` guard.

